From: Hongchan Choi <hongchan@google.com>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 16:40:13 -0700
Subject: [WebAudio] Drop CrossThreadWeakPersistent in media element handler

Previously, MediaElementAudioSourceHandler held a
CrossThreadWeakPersistent<HTMLMediaElement> and called MediaElement()
multiple times on the audio rendering thread during Process() and
ProvideResamplerInput(). Because each call returned a temporary
CrossThreadPersistent that was released immediately, concurrent
main-thread garbage collection of HTMLMediaElement between the initial
check in Process() and subsequent dereferences (including the
unchecked call in ProvideResamplerInput()) could cause the audio
thread to dereference a cleared media element pointer. Locking
CrossThreadWeakPersistent on the audio thread also acquired Oilpan's
global PersistentRegionLock on the real-time rendering path.

Changes:
- Replace CrossThreadWeakPersistent<HTMLMediaElement> with a
  scoped_refptr<WebAudioSourceProviderImpl> guarded by process_lock_,
  decoupling audio-thread rendering from the Oilpan-managed
  HTMLMediaElement so the reference-counted provider remains valid for
  the entire render quantum.
- Pull audio frames directly from WebAudioSourceProviderImpl via a
  stack-allocated channel span array in ProvideInput() without
  audio-thread heap allocations or Oilpan interactions.
- Return silence in WebAudioSourceProviderImpl::ProvideInput() when
  its client has been cleared during media element teardown.
- Reset audio_source_provider_ and multi_channel_resampler_ under
  process_lock_ in Dispose(), OnCurrentSrcChanged(), and invalid
  SetFormat() transitions so main-thread state updates stay
  synchronized with the audio thread.
- Add unit tests verifying audio rendering before and after
  HTMLMediaElement collection and client disconnection.

Fixed: 573836121
Test: blink_unittests --gtest_filter=*MediaElementAudioSourceNodeTest*
Test: blink_platform_unittests --gtest_filter=*WebAudioSourceProvider*
